### Can an abstract class extend another class and implement interfaces?

Yes. The key insight: an abstract class is allowed to have unfinished business. It can declare that it implements an interface without providing the method body.

### How does a layered hierarchy work?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;
import java.util.List;

// Layer 1: base abstract class with state
public abstract class Account {
    protected String accountId;
    protected BigDecimal balance;

    protected Account(String id, BigDecimal bal) {
        this.accountId = id;
        this.balance = bal;
    }

    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) { balance = balance.add(amount); }
    public abstract String getAccountType();
}

// Layer 2: abstract class extends Account AND implements 2 interfaces
public abstract class InterestBearingAccount extends Account implements Auditable, Taxable {
    protected BigDecimal annualRate;

    protected InterestBearingAccount(String id, BigDecimal bal, BigDecimal rate) {
        super(id, bal);
        this.annualRate = rate;
    }

    public BigDecimal calculateMonthlyInterest() {
        return balance.multiply(annualRate).divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(12), 2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    }

    // getAccountType() from Account: still abstract, passed to concrete subclass.
    // Auditable.generateAuditReport(): still abstract, passed to concrete subclass.
    // Taxable.calculateTax(): still abstract, passed to concrete subclass.
    // This class must be abstract because it has unimplemented methods.
}

// Layer 3: concrete class implements ALL remaining abstract methods
public class SavingsAccount extends InterestBearingAccount {
    public SavingsAccount(String id, BigDecimal bal, BigDecimal rate) {
        super(id, bal, rate);
    }

    @Override
    public String getAccountType() { return "SAVINGS"; }

    @Override
    public List<String> generateAuditReport() {
        return List.of("Account: " + accountId, "Balance: Rs." + balance);
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal calculateTax() {
        BigDecimal yearlyInterest = calculateMonthlyInterest().multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(12));
        return yearlyInterest.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.30")); // 30% tax on interest
    }
}
```

### What methods are abstract at each layer?

| Layer | Class | Abstract Methods | Concrete Methods |
|-------|-------|-----------------|------------------|
| 1 | Account | getAccountType() | deposit() |
| 2 | InterestBearingAccount | getAccountType(), generateAuditReport(), calculateTax() | calculateMonthlyInterest() |
| 3 | SavingsAccount | None (all implemented) | Everything above + overrides |

### Can the middle layer partially implement interface methods?

Yes. The abstract class can implement some interface methods and leave others for the concrete subclass.

```java
public abstract class InterestBearingAccount extends Account implements Auditable, Taxable {
    // Implements Auditable.generateAuditReport() here:
    @Override
    public List<String> generateAuditReport() {
        return List.of("Account: " + accountId, "Type: " + getAccountType());
    }

    // Leaves Taxable.calculateTax() abstract: concrete subclass must implement.
}
// Now SavingsAccount only needs: getAccountType() + calculateTax()
```

### When is this pattern useful?

This pattern is useful when multiple concrete classes share intermediate behavior but differ in specifics.

```java
// FixedDepositAccount also extends InterestBearingAccount:
public class FixedDepositAccount extends InterestBearingAccount {
    @Override public String getAccountType() { return "FD"; }
    @Override public List<String> generateAuditReport() { /* FD-specific */ }
    @Override public BigDecimal calculateTax() { /* different tax rules for FD */ }
}
// Both SavingsAccount and FixedDepositAccount share:
// - Account state (accountId, balance, deposit)
// - InterestBearingAccount behavior (calculateMonthlyInterest)
// - Auditable and Taxable capabilities
// But each provides its own getAccountType(), audit report, and tax calculation.
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting that the concrete subclass must implement all remaining abstract methods.

```java
// WRONG: concrete class missing abstract method implementation
// public class SavingsAccount extends InterestBearingAccount {
//     @Override public String getAccountType() { return "SAVINGS"; }
//     // Missing: generateAuditReport() and calculateTax()
//     // Compile error: "SavingsAccount is not abstract and does not override..."
// }
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- This layered pattern maps directly to Domain-Driven Design aggregate hierarchies.
- In JPA, a base entity class can be abstract with @MappedSuperclass, extended by domain-specific abstract classes, and finally by concrete entities.
- In Spring, this pattern is used in AbstractController -> AbstractAnnotationConfigDispatcherServletInitializer hierarchies.
